# Playwright NLP Automation Playbook — v2.0.0

> **What this playbook actually does (plain English):**
> You give the AI agent a live URL and describe a flow in everyday language
> ("log in, add the cheapest item to the cart, and confirm the badge shows 1").
> The agent opens a *real* browser, walks the flow itself, writes a Playwright
> test whose every locator was *seen working live*, runs it, and auto-repairs it
> if it breaks. It then hands you an evidence report mapping each plan step to
> the test code and the artifact that proves it ran.
>
> **What it does NOT do:** It does not invent selectors, does not assume page
> structure, and does not claim a test passed without a trace/screenshot to
> prove it. Absence is reported explicitly, never silently.

---

## Part 0 — Core Contract (Non-Negotiable Rules)

These rules bind every phase below. If a rule cannot be satisfied, the agent
**stops and reports**, it does not improvise.

1. **Live-grounding rule (the prime directive).** Every locator written into a
   `.spec.ts` file MUST correspond to an element the agent actually resolved
   against the live DOM/accessibility tree during execution. No locator may be
   authored from memory, assumption, or static HTML inspection alone.
2. **Resilient-locator hierarchy.** Locators are chosen in this strict priority
   order; the agent only descends a rung when the rung above is genuinely
   unavailable, and it records *why* (see Part 4).
3. **Evidence-only reporting.** Every claim of "pass" must be backed by a trace,
   screenshot, or runner exit code. Every claim of "could not test X" must name
   what was attempted and what was observed — `Not detected — [what was scanned]`.
4. **Zero hardcoding.** No hardcoded waits (`waitForTimeout`), no hardcoded
   ports, no hardcoded credentials, no environment-specific absolute paths. All
   such values come from inputs, fixtures, or config.
5. **Determinism over speed.** Web-first assertions and auto-waiting only. A test
   that flakes is not "done" (see Part 9, the stability gate).
6. **Human-in-the-loop on ambiguity.** When the Planner's understanding of the
   scenario diverges from observed app behavior, or the Healer cannot fix a test
   within the iteration cap, the agent escalates rather than guessing.
7. **Safety boundary.** The browser tool surface is restricted to the minimum
   capability set and an origin allow-list scoped to `target_url`. The
   code-execution / `evaluate` / "unsafe" tool is **never** enabled against an
   untrusted target.

