## Part 1 — Why v2 Exists (Architecture Decision Record)

**v1** instructed the agent to drive the Playwright **MCP server** and emit a
single plain `.js` script, with the live-grounding rule enforced by hand.

**v2** changes the *engine*, not the *philosophy*. Since v1 was authored,
Microsoft shipped first-party **Playwright Test Agents** (Planner / Generator /
Healer, v1.56+) and the **`@playwright/cli`** companion. v2 orchestrates these
official agents instead of re-implementing generation logic.

| Concern | v1 approach | v2 approach | Why it is more robust |
|---|---|---|---|
| Generation engine | Hand-rolled prompt over MCP | Official Planner→Generator→Healer loop | First-party, maintained, upstream-aligned with each Playwright release |
| Locator grounding | Enforced by prompt rule | Enforced by rule **and** by agents operating on a live browser | Agents emit role/text locators because they resolve against the real DOM |
| Self-repair | None | Healer auto-repairs drifted locators with an iteration cap | Drift from a renamed button no longer fails the suite silently |
| Token cost (Copilot) | MCP (~114k tokens/task) | CLI (~27k tokens/task) where filesystem access exists | ~4× cheaper; Copilot has filesystem access |
| Output format | plain `.js` | `.spec.ts` (default) — see decision below | Type-safety, fixtures, parallel isolation, native report/trace |

> **Decision point left to the maintainer (Vivek):** v2 defaults to `.spec.ts`
> because the official agents and the entire 2026 tooling surface (traces,
> fixtures, ARIA snapshots, parallel isolation) assume Playwright Test format.
> If CodeMind has a hard requirement for plain `.js`, see **Part 10, Mode B**,
> which keeps the orchestration but emits a compiled plain-JS artifact. This is
> a deliberate divergence from v1 and is flagged here rather than buried.

